<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-subspace-top" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Subspace topology</title>
  We begin with the following proposition.
  <proposition xml:id="prop-subspace-top">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> a topological space and let <m>Y</m> be a subset of <m>X</m>. Following topologies defined on <m>Y</m> are all equivalent. 
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The topology <m>\tau_1=\{U\cap X:U\text{ an open subset of }X\}</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The <em>coarsest</em> topology <m>\tau_2</m> on <m>Y</m> for which the canonical inclusion <m>i\colon Y\hookrightarrow X</m> is continuous.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    (Universal property of subspace topology). The topology <m>\tau_3</m> on <m>Y</m> satisfying the following property.
                </p>
                <p>
                    For every topological space <m>(Z,\tau_Z)</m> and any function <m>f\colon Z\to Y</m>, <m>f</m> is continuous if and only if <m>i\circ f\colon Z\to X</m> is continuous. See the following diagram.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-universal-property-of-subspace-top">
                    <caption>The universal property of the subspace topology</caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
Z \arrow[d,"f",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"i\circ f"] \amp   \\
Y \arrow[r, hook,"i",labels=below]      \amp X
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <proof>
    <p>
        The equivalence of topologies <m>\tau_1</m> and <m>\tau_2</m> is left as an exercise.
    </p>
    <p>
        We show that <m>\tau_3\subseteq\tau_1</m>. Consider the following diagram, where <m>1_Y\colon Y\to Y</m> is the identity map.
        <figure>
                    <caption></caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
(Y,\tau_1) \arrow[d,"1_Y",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"i\circ 1_Y"] \amp   \\
(Y,\tau_3) \arrow[r,"i",labels=below]      \amp X
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
                </figure>
                Note that <m>i=i\circ 1_Y</m>, and with the topology <m>\tau_1</m> the inclusion <m>i\colon (Y,\tau_1)\to X</m> is continuous. By the universal property of <m>\tau_3</m>, the identity <m>1_Y\colon(Y,\tau_1)\to (Y,\tau_3)</m> is also continuous. Hence, <m>\tau_3\subseteq\tau_1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        We now consider the following.
        <figure>
                    <caption></caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
(Y,\tau_3) \arrow[d,"1_Y",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"i\circ 1_Y"] \amp   \\
(Y,\tau_3) \arrow[r,"i",labels=below]      \amp X
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
        </figure>
        As <m>1_Y\colon (Y,\tau_3)\to (Y,\tau_3)</m> is continuous, using the universal property of <m>\tau_3</m>, we get that <m>i=i\circ 1_Y</m> is also continuous. Since the <m>\tau_1</m> is the coarsest topology that makes inclusion continuous, we have that <m>\tau_1\subseteq\tau_3</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <definition xml:id="def-subspace-top">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>(X,\tau)</m> be a topological space, and let <m>Y</m> be a subset of <m>X</m>. The <em>subspace topology</em> on <m>Y</m> is any of the topologies defined by <xref ref="prop-subspace-top"/>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
        Suppose that <m>Y_\alpha\subseteq X_\alpha</m> and that each <m>Y_\alpha</m> is given the subspace topology. We can consider a product topology on <m>\prod Y_\alpha</m> as well as subspace topology induced by a product topology on <m>\prod X_\alpha</m>. Are these topologies same on <m>\prod Y_\alpha</m>?
    </statement>
    <hint>
    <statement>
        We denote by <m>\tau_p</m> the product topology on <m>\prod Y_\alpha</m> and by <m>\tau_s</m> the subspace topology on <m>\prod Y_\alpha</m>.
        <p>
            For any <m>\alpha</m>, the projection map <m>\pi_\alpha\colon(\prod Y_\alpha,\tau_s)\to Y_\alpha</m> is continuous. Indeed, for an open subset <m>V_\alpha\subseteq Y_\alpha</m>, <m>\pi_\alpha^{-1}\left(V_\alpha\right)=\left\{(y_\beta):y_\beta\in V_\alpha\text{ when }\beta=\alpha\right\}</m> is an open subset with respect to <m>\tau_s</m>. Thus, by the universal property of product topology <m>1\colon\left(\prod Y_\alpha,\tau_s\right)\to\left(\prod Y_\alpha,\tau_p\right)</m> is continuous, i.e., <m>\tau_p\subseteq\tau_s</m>
        </p>
        <p>
               <figure>
                    
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
(\prod Y_\alpha,\tau_s) \arrow[d,"1",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"\pi_\alpha\circ 1"] \amp   \\
(\prod Y_\alpha,\tau_p) \arrow[r, hook,"\pi_\alpha",labels=below]      \amp Y_\alpha
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
                </figure>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </hint>
  </exercise>
  
  <example>
  <title>(Euclidean and product topology on <m>\R^n</m>)</title>
  
  
    <statement>
        <p>
            We consider <m>\R</m> with the usual topology. We show that the product topology on <m>\R\times\R\times\cdots\times\R</m> is same as the Euclidean topology on <m>\R^n</m>. Note that the projection maps <m>\pi_i\colon\R^n\to\R</m> are continuous. Therefore, using the universal property of the product topology we get that an open subset in the product topology is open in the Euclidean topology.
        </p>
        <p>
            <figure>
                    
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            
\begin{tikzcd}
\R^n \arrow[d,"1",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"\pi_i\circ 1"] \amp   \\
\R\times\R\times\cdots\times\R \arrow[r,"\pi_i",labels=below]      \amp \R
\end{tikzcd}
                        </latex-image>
                        
                    </image>
                </figure>
        </p>
        <p>
            A direct way to see this is following. Suppose that <m>v=\left(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n\right)\in\R^n</m>. Consider an open subset <m>U=(x_1-\epsilon/\sqrt{n},x_1+\epsilon/\sqrt{n})\times\cdots\times(x_n-\epsilon/\sqrt{n},x_n+\epsilon/\sqrt{n})</m> in the product topology. Note that <m>B(v,\epsilon)\subset U</m>. This shows that a basic open subset in the product topology is an union of open balls in the euclidean topology.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
</section>